      "content": "Yes, $(10,5)$ is the first point: move to $10$ on the horizontal axis, then up to $5$ on the vertical axis. Now use the same order for every row: $(x,y)$.\n\n**Final Answer:** Draw a scatter plot with these 10 points:\n$$(10,5),\\ (3,5),\\ (20,9),\\ (14,7),\\ (17,6),\\ (9,6),\\ (12,8),\\ (18,11),\\ (15,10),\\ (19,12).$$\n\nLabel the horizontal axis **Number of items** and the vertical axis **Time (minutes)**. Use the grid’s scales, plot each point carefully, and do not join the points with lines.\n\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $\\text{plotted point}=(x,y)$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $x$: the number of items in the order; it is read from the horizontal axis.\n  * $y$: the packing time in minutes; it is read from the vertical axis.\n* **Why we use it:** Each pair keeps the two measurements for one order together. Plot $x$ first, then move vertically to its matching $y$ value to place the point.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** This question is marked for correctly plotted points: the memo checks 3 points, then 6 points, then all 10. There is no formula-substitution mark for this scatter plot. Avoid swapping the coordinates or axes, and include **minutes** in the vertical-axis label."
